<article class="env-proposition" id="052N">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/052N">Proposition <span data-tag="052N">3.5.3.23</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a Kan complex and let $n$ be an integer. Then the $n$-coskeleton $\operatorname{cosk}_{n}( X )$ is also a Kan complex. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $m$ be a positive integer. Fix an integer $0 \leq i \leq m$ and a morphism of simplicial sets $\sigma _0: \Lambda ^{m}_{i} \rightarrow \operatorname{cosk}_{n}(X)$; we wish to show that $\sigma _0$ can be extended to an $m$-simplex of $\operatorname{cosk}_ n(X)$. Using Remark <a data-tag="052L" href="/tag/052L">3.5.3.21</a>, we can identify $\sigma _0$ with a morphism of simplicial sets $f_0: \operatorname{sk}_{n}( \Lambda ^{m}_{i} ) \rightarrow X$; we wish to show that $f_0$ can be extended to the $n$-skeleton of $\Delta ^{m}$. If $n &lt; m-1$, then $\operatorname{sk}_{n}( \Lambda ^{m}_{i} ) = \operatorname{sk}_{n}( \Delta ^{m} )$ and there is nothing to prove. We may therefore assume that $n \geq m-1$, so that $\operatorname{sk}_{n}( \Lambda ^{m}_{i} ) = \Lambda ^{m}_{i}$. In this case, our assumption that $X$ is a Kan complex guarantees that $f_0$ can be extended to an $n$-simplex of $X$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
